## Repo layout

```
index.html              the portfolio website (GitHub Pages serves this)
projects/
  <project>/
    README.md           write-up for that project
    images/             photos, CAD, and drawings used by the README and the site
.nojekyll               tells GitHub Pages to serve files as-is
```

The website and the READMEs share the same images, so a photo you add once appears in both.

## Publishing

1. Create a new repository on GitHub (for example `portfolio`).
2. Upload these files, or from this folder run:
   ```bash
   git init
   git add .
   git commit -m "Add project portfolio"
   git branch -M main
   git remote add origin https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/portfolio.git
   git push -u origin main
   ```
3. In the repo, open **Settings > Pages**, set **Source** to *Deploy from a branch*, choose `main` and `/ (root)`, and save.
4. After a minute the site is live at `https://YOUR-USERNAME.github.io/portfolio/`. Name the repo `YOUR-USERNAME.github.io` to get the shorter `https://YOUR-USERNAME.github.io/` instead.

## Personalizing

- In `index.html`, replace `Your Name`, the email address, and the GitHub link (search for `YOUR-`).
